  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nThere will be some lower chances for some showers and storms to \ndevelop over northeastern SD and west central MN early this morning \nas a front passes through. After the front moves through, high \nsurface pressure will be moving in, and will stick around through \nthe day. In combination with the lack of moisture in the atmosphere, \nthe high pressure will help to keep showers and storms from \ndeveloping. Colder air has moved in behind that boundary and will \nhelp to keep temperatures slightly cooler than yesterday, with highs \nin the upper 70s to mid 80s. The upper-levels continue to have \nnorthwest flow over SD through the weekend and into next week. This \nnorthwest flow will continue to move in wildfire smoke aloft from \nthe fires out in the Pacific Northwest. However, with very little \nmixing, this smoke will not make it to the surface and only create \nhazy skies. \n\nThere will be some upper-level shortwaves that move over \nSD in the coming days. These shortwaves will provide some energy \nthat could help showers and storms to develop over central and \nnortheastern SD. The next chance for one of these shortwaves will be \nFriday night into Saturday morning. Additionally, a low level jet \nwill be developing over western and central SD, and will help with \nthe development of storms and showers. Models have been fairly \nconsistent that something could develop during that time over \nportions of south central SD. However, there is some variability in \ntiming and how widespread precipitation will be due to the lack of \nmoisture in the atmosphere and where/when the models bring some \nmoisture up into central SD. With the variability in the models as \nwell as concerns about if, when, and where there will be moisture, \nthere continues to be lower confidence for storms Friday night into \nSaturday morning. If storms are able to develop, there is a marginal \nrick (level 1 of 5) for them to become severe, with the main\nhazards of hail up to 1 inch and wind gusts of 60 mph possible.\n\nTemperatures will warm slightly Saturday and Sunday, with highs in \nthe low 80s to low 90s. The next big chance for showers and storms \nto occur is Sunday evening into Monday morning. There looks to be \nsome more moisture moving into SD aloft Sunday. That, in combination \nwith a boundary moving through and a bit of instability from the \nwarmer temperatures, will help to develop showers and storms. Models \nstill vary the location and timing of the showers and storms, which \nlowers confidence slightly. Despite this lower confidence, there is \nstill a 35-55% chance of precipitation into Monday morning.",
